---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { celestial, ocean, photos, scenes } from '../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Relationship Astrology', href: '/explore/relationships/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Relationship Astrology — synastry and composite charts"
  description="How relationship astrology works: synastry overlays two birth charts; the composite chart describes the relationship itself. What compatibility can and cannot mean."
  path="/explore/relationships/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
      <h1>Relationship astrology</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Two people, two charts — and two honest questions: how do these charts activate each
        other, and what is the relationship itself asking for?
      </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.relationship.src}
        srcset={scenes.relationship.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.relationship.alt}
        width={scenes.relationship.width}
        height={scenes.relationship.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Synastry: the overlay</h2>
      <p>
        Synastry lays one chart over the other and reads the aspects between them. Your Moon on
        their Venus: affection flows easily. Their Saturn square your Sun: they steady you — and
        sometimes dampen you — and the relationship will keep renegotiating that line. Synastry
        excels at explaining <em>why it feels the way it feels</em>: the instant ease, the
        recurring argument that is somehow always the same argument.
      </p>
      <h2>The composite: the third thing</h2>
      <p>
        A composite chart is built from the midpoints of both charts, and read as the relationship
        itself — a third entity with its own character, needs, and seasons. Couples often
        recognize it instantly: "that's not quite me, and not quite them — that's <em>us</em>."
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.clearedTable} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What compatibility isn't</h2>
      <p>
        No chart pairing is doomed, and none is guaranteed. "Hard" synastry builds some of the
        most durable partnerships — friction, honestly handled, becomes traction. What the charts
        genuinely offer is a precise vocabulary for the dynamics: where ease lives, where the work
        is, and what the work is <em>about</em>. Whether to do the work remains what it always
        was — a choice two people make.
      </p>
      <h2>Beyond romance</h2>
      <p>
        The same techniques read any significant pair: business partners, parent and child,
        siblings, close friends. Anywhere two lives are intertwined, the charts have something
        useful to say. That's the territory of the
        <a href="/readings/relationship/">Relationship Consultation</a> — which, for the record,
        requires both people's birth data brought in good faith, and works best with both of you
        there.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Two charts, one conversation"
    body="The relationship reading looks at synastry and composite together — for couples, families, friends, and business partners."
    buttonLabel="About the Relationship Consultation"
    buttonHref="/readings/relationship/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
